# Human corrections to B1, Quasqueton upper level.
# APPLIED LAST by build.py, so these always win over the classifier.
# Format: what  x  y  orient  value  who  note      (see corrections.tsv)
#
# --- furniture drawn inside a room, read as structure -------------------------
# Both of these were found by the room-size cross-check, not by reachability.
# Note they fail differently: one fabricates a wall, the other a DOOR -- and a
# fabricated door is still passable, so no connectivity check can see it.
edge	2	21	V	open	dl	area 17: things drawn in the room, read as a wall (thick=10)
edge	6	19	H	open	dl	area 18: things drawn in the NE corner, read as a door (hole=47)
edge	5	20	V	open	dl	area 18: the same NE-corner furniture, now read as a door at boxr=0.50
edge	25	8	H	open	dl	area 28: the drawn sacrifice pit ("a circular depression... 5ft across") read as a door
#
# --- area 9, WIZARD'S LABORATORY ------------------------------------------------
# The room is x11-15, y26-28 (5x3 = 15 squares), matching the text's "50' by 30'".
# It is densely furnished -- tables, shelving, containers, glassware -- and the
# drawings produced SIX false doors and one false wall inside it, splitting the
# room into a 3x3 fragment. Every one of these is interior furniture.
edge	11	26	V	open	dl	area 9: furniture read as a door (hole=51)
edge	11	28	V	open	dl	area 9: furniture read as a door (hole=111)
edge	12	28	V	open	dl	area 9: furniture read as a door (hole=134)
edge	11	26	H	open	dl	area 9: furniture read as a door (hole=86)
edge	12	26	H	open	dl	area 9: furniture read as a wall (thick=6)
edge	11	27	H	open	dl	area 9: furniture read as a door (hole=47)
# The laboratory's secret entrance. Read as a plain wall (thick=14) with no glyph
# at all -- the marker detector never proposed it, and reachability could not have
# found it either because the room has a second, ordinary door.
edge	14	28	H	secret	dl	area 9: secret door in the north wall of (14,28)
#
# --- area 25, ROGAHN'S CHAMBER (the party is standing here) ----------------------
# An irregular lobed chamber, x31-34 y11-15. Three S markers on the sheet.
edge	32	10	H	secret	dl	area 25: S marker on the south wall (read as wall, thick=17)
edge	33	10	H	secret	dl	area 25: second S marker on the south wall (read as wall, thick=14)
edge	31	12	H	secret	dl	area 25: S marker on the west diagonal stub (read as a plain door)
edge	32	13	V	open	dl	area 25: furniture between two of its own squares, read as a door
#
# --- secret doors the module text names but never locates ------------------------
# Confirmed against the sheet by the player, 2026-08-25. All three read as plain
# walls at thickness 14-18 with NO glyph proposed by the detector, and none could
# be corroborated by reachability because each room has another way in. This is
# the class of fault that only the text sweep finds.
edge	8	25	V	secret	dl	area 10 STOREROOM: east wall of (8,25) — "hidden by a secret door"
edge	33	6	H	secret	dl	area 24 MISTRESS' CHAMBER: north wall of (33,6)
edge	30	19	V	secret	dl	area 32 ADVISOR'S CHAMBER: its west wall — "access only via a secret door... to the Room of Pools (31)". The party found this one in play.
#
# --- alcove pair 2, the one-way secret door the party has actually used --------
# Found while migrating play state into campaign/world_changes.tsv: the party
# went through this boundary at the end of expedition 3 and the map database
# did not have it. It reads as a plain wall (thick=15) though it carries a hole
# of 15, and reachability could never have found it because both sides are
# reachable by the ordinary route. Only the play record caught it.
# The corridor east of it is the west dead end; the door is obvious from that
# side and unnoticeable from the entrance-corridor face, hence one_way.
edge	17	13	V	one_way	dl	area 1: one-way secret door, back of alcove pair 2; operable from the EAST (dead-end) side only
#
# --- area 27: furniture on the room's east side read as two doors -------------
# Found in play 2026-08-25 while the party was approaching the room. Both edges
# classified 'door' at thickness 13-14 with holes of 283 and 95; B1's real doors
# measure thickness 4-11 with holes of 62-174, so these are far too heavy and
# the 283 is nearly twice the largest genuine door on the level. The sheet draws
# a large oval object here containing two smaller glyphs -- a drawn furnishing
# sitting on the line, not an opening.
# Neither could be caught by reachability (a fabricated door stays passable) nor
# by the extent check (the room has another way in). Only the measurements and
# the page render found them, which is the third time this class of error has
# turned up in a furnished room.
edge	31	8	V	open	dl	area 27: drawn furnishing on the east side read as a door (thick=13 hole=283)
edge	31	9	V	open	dl	area 27: the same furnishing, second glyph read as a door (thick=14 hole=95)
# The same drawn object, north and south arcs. Found 2026-08-26 when dmap.py
# path reported the party "crossing a door" walking across open floor inside
# area 27. THICKNESS 2 -- that is printed-ruling territory (B1 rulings measure
# 0-3, walls 7-18), so these are not even wall segments, let alone doors. The
# neighbouring boundaries without the drawing over them read thickness 1 'open'.
# The oval was read as a door on ALL FOUR sides; the first pass caught only the
# two on its west face.
edge	32	9	H	open	dl	area 27: north arc of the drawn dais read as a door (thick=2 hole=95)
edge	32	7	H	open	dl	area 27: south arc of the drawn dais read as a door (thick=2 hole=69)
#
# --- area 28: a fourth fabricated door, same signature -------------------------
# Found in play 2026-08-27. Classified 'door' at THICKNESS 2 with a hole of 60.
# Two is printed-ruling territory (B1 rulings 0-3, walls 7-18); the genuine door
# ten feet away at (26,8,V) measures thickness 9. It also fails the architecture
# test: it would be an internal door between two squares that BOTH already open
# straight into the worship area, which is not a door, it is a doorway with a
# line drawn across it.
# Area 28 has now produced TWO false doors from its drawings -- this one and the
# sacrifice pit at (25,8,H) -- which makes it the third furnished room in a row
# to do so, after areas 9 and 27.
edge	26	8	H	open	dl	area 28: printed ruling read as a door (thick=2 hole=60); an internal door between two squares both open to the same room
# --- area 27, a fifth fabricated door, found by walking through it -------------
# dmap.py path reported the party "crossing a door" between two squares that are
# BOTH inside the throne room. Thickness 1 -- a printed grid ruling, not a wall.
# That is the fifth of this session and the second caught by the path command
# rather than by any classifier.
edge	27	8	H	open	dl	area 27: printed ruling read as a door (thick=1 hole=54), between two squares of the same room
#
# --- area 25: the LOBED OUTLINE read as five doors ----------------------------
# Player-corrected 2026-08-28. Rogahn's Chamber is a four-lobed clover and its
# curving walls cross grid lines constantly; every crossing that enclosed white
# fired the door test. The room's ONLY entrances are its three secret doors --
# one mid-west, two in the south wall -- which the player reads directly off the
# sheet and which the three S markers confirm.
# (34,13) is a near-solid outcrop between two lobes: open=0.18 with rock on all
# four sides. It cannot be a doorway to anywhere.
# (31,13) is the square where the west corridor ends at a DIAGONAL wall carrying
# the S marker; the corridor must reach the room through that secret door and
# nowhere else, so its other two boundaries are wall.
edge	31	12	V	wall	dl	area 25: lobe outline read as an opening; the west corridor reaches the room ONLY through the secret door at (31,12,H)
edge	31	13	H	wall	dl	area 25: lobe outline read as a door (thick=10 hole=140)
edge	33	13	V	wall	dl	area 25: lobe outline read as a door (thick=7 hole=136)
edge	34	13	H	wall	dl	area 25: lobe outline round the (34,13) outcrop read as a door (thick=11 hole=136)
edge	34	12	H	wall	dl	area 25: same outcrop, south side (thick=23 hole=145)
edge	31	13	V	wall	dl	area 25: the west corridor reaches the room ONLY through the secret door at (31,12,H) — this was the edge I meant to correct and I indexed the wrong one
edge	31	11	V	wall	dl	area 25: lobe outline on the north-west, read as an opening
#
# --- area 24: furniture read as walls, halving the room ------------------------
# Player-corrected 2026-08-28. The MISTRESS' CHAMBER is 20 ft by 40 (x32-33,
# y3-6). The extraction gave it 20 by 20 because the drawn bed against the west
# wall was read as rock: (32,4) came out 'mixed' at open=0.64 with rock on its
# north and west sides, which fabricated a wall on two boundaries and a door on
# a third, and the 2x2 extent test then stopped at y=5.
# NOTE: (32,6) is left as 'mixed' DELIBERATELY. That is the north-west corner,
# and the module says the water basin there "is sculpted from the same rock
# which forms the wall of the room... this protrusion is an integral part of the
# wall itself." The classifier is RIGHT about that square — it really is part
# rock. Good check on the method.
cell	32	4	-	open	dl	area 24: the drawn walnut bed read as rock (open=0.64, sides=NW)
edge	32	4	H	open	dl	area 24: bed read as a wall (thick=7 hole=27)
edge	32	4	V	open	dl	area 24: same bed, read as a wall (thick=6 hole=0)
edge	32	3	H	open	dl	area 24: furniture read as a door (thick=6 hole=41)
#
# --- area 24: furniture that is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a door -----------------
# Player-corrected 2026-08-28, and this pair is the important one. The sheet has
# exactly ONE door in area 24's south wall, on the east side, at (33,2,H). The
# extraction also produced a door in the middle of the south wall and one in the
# west wall. Both are the drawn nightstand/chest furniture beside the bed.
# THESE TWO WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN CAUGHT BY THE THICKNESS FLAG. They measure 9 and
# 10 with holes of 134 -- dead centre of the genuine 4-11 door band, and holes
# in the normal 62-187 range. Nothing in the classifier's own output separates
# them from a real door. Only the page render and the player's reading did.
edge	32	2	H	wall	dl	area 24: drawn furniture read as a door in the south wall (thick=9 hole=134) — indistinguishable by measurement
edge	31	3	V	wall	dl	area 24: drawn furniture read as a door in the west wall (thick=10 hole=134) — indistinguishable by measurement
edge	29	2	H	wall	dl	south corridor: furniture drawn on the room side only, read as a door — see the one-sided test in lab_notebook.md
#
# --- area 23: the SIXTH furnished room to be mis-extracted ---------------------
# 2026-08-28. STORAGE ROOM is 20 ft by 30 (x29-30, y3-5) on the sheet. The
# extraction gave it TWO squares. It is a furniture store -- three oak tables, a
# pile of chairs, fourteen stools stacked against the walls, a workbench with a
# vise -- and essentially all of it read as structure: one interior wall, two
# interior doors and a part-rock square, none of which exist.
# Every one of these fails the one-sided test: the drawn objects sit inside the
# room and never cross a wall band.
cell	30	3	-	open	dl	area 23: stacked furniture read as rock (open=0.66, sides=SE)
edge	29	5	V	open	dl	area 23: a drawn table read as an interior wall (thick=7 hole=12)
edge	30	3	H	open	dl	area 23: furniture read as an interior door (thick=2 hole=80)
edge	29	3	V	open	dl	area 23: furniture read as an interior door (thick=3 hole=80)
edge	21	11	H	wall	claude	area 30 stair limb: the LANDING at the head of the stairs read as a door (hole=347 aspect=2.2 vs 95-187 for a real B1 door; wall=2 offset=0.23). Flagged by doorcheck. The limb is closed at its north end -- the stairway is reached only from area 30.
edge	21	11	V	wall	claude	area 30 stair limb: east wall read as `stairs` because the tread ladder inside (21,11) put slots+hole on the boundary (thick=12 gap=0 slots=1). The 200 dpi render shows an unbroken bar at x=22 from y=10 to y=12. The limb is entered ONLY from area 30 via (21,9)->(21,10).
edge	26	5	V	wall	claude	area 29: a small drawn furnishing against the INSIDE of the east wall read as a door (hole=69 vs 95-187 for a real B1 door; offset=0.22 cells off the bar). Flagged by doorcheck before the party saw it. The south corridor (27,4)-(27,6) has NO door in its west wall.
edge	25	4	H	wall	claude	area 29 CAPTAIN'S CHAMBER: a drawn chest/bench against the INSIDE of the south wall read as `stairs` (thick=13 slots=1 hole=89, offset 0.15 off the bar). The south wall is an unbroken bar from x=23 to x=27 on the 200 dpi render.
edge	26	4	H	wall	claude	area 29: more drawn furniture against the inside of the same south wall read as a door (hole=160 -- door-sized! -- but offset 0.22 cells off the bar). Only the one-sided test catches this one; hole and thickness both look like a genuine door.
edge	26	5	H	open	claude	area 29 INTERIOR: a small furnishing read as a door in the middle of the room (thick=1.0, wall=1.0 -- a graph-paper ruling, no wall there at all).
cell	26	5	-	open	claude	area 29 floor, read as `mixed` because furniture is drawn on it. The room is 30x20 (x24-26, y5-6); the label "29" straddles x=26 and (26,5)/(26,6) are inside the drawn outline.
edge	25	13	H	wall	claude	area 31 ROOM OF POOLS: the circled letter "b" — a POOL MARKER printed just inside the room — read as a door (hole=62 vs 95-187 real; offset 0.21 off an unbroken bar). Same failure as the secret-door markers: the circle is drawn astride the wall, touches the rock mass and is never separated into the ink layer. The real door off this corridor is (28,13,H): hole 145, offset 0.11. Calibration: (27,12,H), physically walked through, is hole 174 offset 0.04.
edge	22	22	H	wall	claude	a small drawn object in the SW corner of (22,23) read as a door. thick 9.0, gap 0.00 — the boundary is fully blocked — against gap 0.75 for the real door at (27,12,H). hole 66 vs 95-193 for genuine B1 doors, offset 0.16 off the bar.
edge	21	23	V	wall	claude	the SAME glyph as (22,22,H): both report hole 66 because the object sits in the corner where the two boundary boxes overlap, so one piece of furniture was counted as two doors. thick 8.5, gap 0.00, offset 0.23.
